# Steam Deck packages INSTALLED ON EVERY TARGET.
#
# T5-fork-plan.md §3 seam S1: this list is merged into the runtime's own
# omarchy-base.packages *before* build-iso.sh copies it to
# airootfs/usr/share/omarchy-iso/, so one edit reaches all three consumers at
# once -- the offline mirror's `pacman -Syw`, the target install's
# `_runtime_package_list`, and the `expected-packages` denominator the install
# dashboard draws its progress bar from. Appending anywhere later desyncs them.
#
# ⚠️ NO REPO-QUALIFIED NAMES IN THIS FILE. Entries here are resolved twice
# against the OFFLINE mirror, whose pacman.conf declares exactly one repo
# ([offline], configs/pacman-offline.conf) -- once by build-iso.sh's
# resolve_expected_packages and once by pacstrap at install time. A
# `jupiter-staging/foo` entry aborts both. Repo-qualified names belong in
# deck-mirror.packages, which is only ever resolved against the online repos.
# deck-nvidia-dry-run.sh refuses the build if one appears here.
#
# Keep this list SHORT. Anything that only needs to be *available* belongs in
# deck-mirror.packages and is installed by the omarchy-deck package (seam S4);
# anything that touches the boot chain is T1/T5f's, not this file's.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The two vulkan-driver pins -- docs/PROGRESS.md §3.8
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `steam` depends on the VIRTUAL packages `vulkan-driver` and
# `lib32-vulkan-driver`. With no AMD provider selected, pacman picks the NVIDIA
# stack on its own and nothing errors: several hundred MB of driver that cannot
# run on this hardware.
#
# 🔴 §3.8's list of what appears is incomplete, MEASURED 2026-08-12 against the
# live repos: it names nvidia-utils, egl-wayland, egl-gbm and egl-x11, and the
# actual set is six -- `lib32-nvidia-utils` and `egl-wayland2` as well.
#
#   pacman -S steam                        -> egl-gbm egl-wayland egl-wayland2
#                                             egl-x11 lib32-nvidia-utils
#                                             nvidia-utils     (6)
#   pacman -S steam lib32-vulkan-radeon    -> egl-gbm egl-wayland egl-wayland2
#                                             egl-x11 nvidia-utils   (5)
#   pacman -S steam vulkan-radeon lib32-vulkan-radeon         -> none  (0)
#
# ⚠️ And §3.8's "naming lib32-vulkan-radeon explicitly drops them to zero" is
# TRUE ONLY BY ACCIDENT OF RESOLUTION ORDER -- do not lean on it. The 32-bit
# package hard-depends on the 64-bit one, so `lib32-vulkan-radeon` alone does
# eventually put `vulkan-radeon` in the transaction. Whether that happens
# BEFORE pacman has to pick a provider for steam's separate 64-bit
# `vulkan-driver` dependency depends on the order the resolver reaches them:
# in the isolated two-package transaction above it does not (nvidia-utils is
# chosen anyway, middle row), and in the full ISO target set it does. Naming
# both is what makes the outcome independent of that order.
#
# Consequence for anyone mutation-testing this file: deleting the
# `lib32-vulkan-radeon` line makes deck-nvidia-dry-run.sh fail the build, but
# deleting `vulkan-radeon` currently does NOT, because the full-set ordering
# happens to save it. That is a behaviour-preserving mutation, not a hole in
# the guard -- the line is here for determinism, and the guard is what notices
# if the ordering ever stops being kind.
#
# Both resolve to ARCH's build, not Valve's, because the Valve repos are
# appended last -- deliberate, on measured evidence (docs/PROGRESS.md §5.13,
# docs/findings/P16-repo-overlap-audit.md: 101 names overlap and Valve's is
# older in 50 of them, the whole mesa/vulkan stack included). The test Deck
# runs Arch's vulkan-radeon with Gaming Mode working.
vulkan-radeon
lib32-vulkan-radeon

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Our own package -- T5-fork-plan.md §3 seam S4
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Built by us, from pkgbuilds/omarchy-deck/, straight into the ISO's offline
# mirror. It exists in NO online repository, which makes it different from
# every other entry in this file and is why the patched build-iso.sh has to
# know about it in four separate places (build it / strip it from the online
# `pacman -Syw` / add it back to the prune keep-set / exclude it from
# deck-nvidia-dry-run.sh's online resolve). Those four all read one
# declaration, derived from the pkgbuilds/ directory listing itself.
#
# It is safe HERE, in the install list, precisely because this file is
# resolved against the OFFLINE mirror -- by resolve_expected_packages after
# repo-add, and by pacstrap at install time -- and the package is in that
# mirror by then. Not repo-qualified, per this file's header: the offline
# pacman.conf declares one repo and a `foo/bar` entry aborts both resolves.
#
# 🔴 If you delete this line, the pipeline still builds a package and still
# carries it in the mirror -- and nothing installs it. That failure is silent
# on the build side and only visible on the target, which is what
# test/unit/test-omarchy-deck-package.sh exists to catch in seconds.
omarchy-deck

# --- Gaming Mode's session file (§14.6) ---------------------------------------
#
# 🔴 This line is what puts `/usr/share/wayland-sessions/gamescope-wayland.desktop`
# on the target, which is the file deck_autologin.py's GAMING_SESSION looks for.
# Without it the install completes and lands in DESKTOP mode -- the §14.6 gap.
#
# It said `gamescope-session` from 2026-08-14 (commit 56c6578) until 2026-08-15.
# That package EXISTS IN NO VALVE REPO -- measured against the package databases
# of all four (jupiter, jupiter-staging, holo, holo-staging): none ships any
# `gamescope-session*`. The name was never build-validated, because the last real
# ISO build predated it; the first build that tried it died on
# `error: target not found: gamescope-session`.
#
# The session file is shipped by the gamescope PACKAGE ITSELF -- but only Valve's
# build of it. Measured from the repo file-lists 2026-08-15:
#   jupiter-staging/gamescope 3.16.25-3  ->  usr/share/wayland-sessions/gamescope-wayland.desktop
#   extra/gamescope           3.16.25-1  ->  no wayland-sessions/ entry at all
# deck-mirror.packages already pulls the Valve build into the offline mirror as
# `jupiter-staging/gamescope` (qualified there, per §5.13). Nothing installed it:
# omarchy-deck does not depend on gamescope and no other list named it, so the
# mirror carried the compositor and the target never got it.
#
# Bare, NOT repo-qualified -- this file is resolved twice against the offline
# mirror, whose pacman.conf declares one repo; a `jupiter-staging/` prefix here
# aborts both resolves (this file's header, and deck-nvidia-dry-run.sh fails the
# build on it). One repo means one candidate: Valve's, the one the mirror kept.
gamescope
